You've got peanut butter and jelly, cookies and milk, and now Mastra and Mastra Observability! 😏 It's the natural pairing for agents that need traces, metrics, and evals, so you can keep improving them after they ship.


Mastra Observability is a hosted platform that connects directly to your Mastra framework project. It gives you and your team one place to see what your agents are doing in production, investigate problems, and run the eval loop that makes them better, so you don't need a separate observability/evals platform.


Here's how it works:


## Connect your project


Connect your project and Mastra automatically traces your agent runs, so you can understand how users interact with your product and investigate when something goes wrong.


```text
import   {   Mastra   }   from   '  @mastra/core/mastra  '  ;
import   {
Observability,
MastraPlatformExporter,
SensitiveDataFilter,
}   from   '  @mastra/observability  '  ;


export   const   mastra   =   new   Mastra  ({
// Automatically trace every agent, workflow, and tool
observability:   new   Observability  ({
configs: {
default: {
serviceName:   '  my-app  '  ,
// Mastra batches traces and sends them to the platform
// asynchronously in the background.
exporters: [  new   MastraPlatformExporter  ()],
// Redact sensitive fields before exporting traces
spanOutputProcessors: [  new   SensitiveDataFilter  ()],
},
},
}),
});
```


note


First,[create a Mastra account](https://projects.mastra.ai/) , then follow the[getting started guide](https://mastra.ai/docs/mastra-platform/observability) to connect your Mastra project.


## Understand every agent run


Search and filter traces to find the runs that matter, then inspect model and tool calls to see exactly where time and tokens were spent, and what they cost.


[Thread view](https://mastra.ai/blog/introducing-thread-view) (pictured above) puts each run in the context of the full conversation. You and your non-technical teammates can start with the messages and drill into the steps behind them when something looks off - no need to decode a raw span tree first.


## Monitor performance, cost, and correctness


Run online scorers on a sample of your traces to track correctness automatically, then watch the trends over time on a[dashboard](https://mastra.ai/docs/observability/metrics/overview) alongside the performance and cost metrics you already care about.


## Build an eval loop


Invite your team to review conversations, annotate traces, and curate datasets for your[eval loop](https://mastra.ai/docs/guides/build-an-eval-loop) .


Run an[experiment](https://mastra.ai/docs/evals/experiments) against your dataset and compare versions of your agent on quality, cost, and latency.


Here I’m comparing results to see if I can downgrade to nano and save on cost without degrading performance (spoiler: I can’t!)


## Use AI to improve agents


As[agent harnesses](https://mastra.ai/docs/harness/overview) take on bigger jobs and rack up longer runs, there’s a lot more trace data to make sense of.


Mastra’s[query traces CLI](https://mastra.ai/docs/mastra-platform/observability#query-observability-data) gives your coding agent a way to investigate slow, expensive, or failed runs and help fix the underlying issues.


